rs  do you think  well i think what we see is that the  overwhelming power of the metoo movement  just continues to endure and even after  all of the obstacles even after a trial  a few years ago that did not result in a  conviction the  the  there is very little that is more  ultimately powerful  than these victims being willing to tell  their stories and they ultimately  prevailed you know i spoke to tarana  burke a few weeks ago about her new book  and what she said is  we  we have a problem in that we tend to see  the metoo movement as a tennis match  like when cuomo leaves office we say  okay you know one point for the women  for the victims when cosby gets freed we  say oh you know subtract one point from  the metoo movement and it's time to  even while that score keeping is  somewhat inevitable the bigger question  is you know what is me too making  possible and i think we see that in this  trial not only in the way the victims  ultimately prevailed but also in the  fact that there was kind of a new legal  theory that was used to bring this case  and you had a set of prosecutors who  seemed very determined to find a way to  see justice for the victims  all right jody cantor thank you so much  and he still has other cases pending we  thank you for your time this morning
 

